[Music]  looking back today we you know we say  hey we buy the UFC for two million  dollars for you say oh you stole it  I'm not sure we did I'm not sure it was  worth two million when we bought it  Lorenzo Fertitta is the CEO of the UFC  the Ultimate Fighting Championship which  is currently valued at about four  billion dollars he owns it along with  his older brother Frank Fertitta and  their high school buddy Dana White  [Music]  growing up in Las Vegas where there were  no professional sports teams  Lorenzo got to watch a lot of boxing  instead I was really able to see some of  the greatest fights in boxing history  Lorenzo's father started station casinos  in the 1970s Lorenzo and his brother  took the company public in 1993 when he  had just finished grad school so you're  a millionaire at 24 yes yes then a few  years later he bumped into his high  school friend Dana White at a wedding I  think we probably talked to each other  27 times a day since that day so one day  Lorenzo Frank and Dana all went down to  New Orleans to watch a big UFC fight  nobody even knew the UFC was in town and  that's how small as an event that it was  at the time there was no organization  there was no buzz I wanted to go buy  like a t-shirt buy a program there was  nothing but there was nothing there  and it was really that moment and when  we were sitting in our seats at that  fight when me Frank and Dana sitting in  a row we looked at each other and said  either there's something extremely wrong  with us wanting to be here to watch  these guys fight in the Octagon or this  is not a very well-executed business and  there's a lot more opportunity here the  UFC wasn't just unpopular it was also  banned by almost every state athletic  commission and no cable networks would  run the fights the owner of Bob merits  was looking for money to keep the  business alive so I cold called Bob it  was a very short conversation he said he  was looking for a million dollars for  half the business so I talked to Frank  and called him back and said we're not  interested in having partners I'll offer  to buy the whole thing four to nine but  two million dollars didn't get them much  more than a wooden octagon in some  fighter contracts you know for the the  next I would say five years it  is a real grind the brothers spent forty  million dollars trying to make the sport  safer and more palatable we had so much  a pushback from networks when we would  go pitch to them and say hey we own the  USC we want to put fights on TV  how many noes did you get every you  count the network's everybody I mean we  literally got to the point where if we  could have put it on the Food Network we  would have put it on there I mean we  knew that just going out selling fights  wasn't working so we had to change our  strategy and that's where we came up  with the idea of The Ultimate Fighter  [Music]  since they couldn't get networks to pick  up regular fights they made a reality  show where each episode ended in a fight  we like to say it was our Trojan horse  Dana White hosted and the overall winner  got a contract with the UFC the Ultimate  Fighter ended up being the turning point  for the company but to get it on the air  they bankrolled the entire production  themselves and gave it to Spike TV to  run for free and because we paid for it  we owned all the rights that was the key  moment because even today one of the  real points of value in the UFC that we  own a hundred percent of everything  the USC today has earned itself a spot  as a primetime sport and it is watched  by millions around the globe the great  thing about our sport is that everybody  understands fighting and we have the  ability because of that to really appeal  to everyone around the world well we own  the UFC which is the strongest brand in  fighting  [Music]

